reens including **HCT116, HEK293T, HepG2, iPSC, Jurkat resting/active, and iPSC Multi-Diff**. Includes a stacked bar plot of cell counts in millions and a **UMAP2/UMAP1** embedding labeled “Cell x Screen,” colored by cell/screen type.\\n- **B:** X-Cell architecture workflow. Inputs from **X-Atlas/Pisces** feed gene perturbation information, prior knowledge sources such as **LLM/ESM2, STRING, DepMap, JUMP-cell painting, scGPT**, and gene-token/expression representations into a diffusion language-model architecture. The diagram highlights control encoding, perturbation conditioning, cross-attention, self-attention, and applications: **context generalization, perturbation prediction, and mechanism of action**.\\n- **C:** Training/inference schematic for **Gene X knockdown**, showing control input, intermediate prediction/remasking, and final predicted expression with control vs predicted color legend.\\n- **D:** Bar chart comparing model parameter counts in billions. X-Cell is labeled **55M 2026**, X-Cell-Ultra **4.9B 2026**, and other models include scGPT, Geneformer V2, STATE-SE, scFoundation, TranscriptFormer, and Tahoe-x1. A visible annotation indicates **92x** scaling from X-Cell to X-Cell-Ultra.\\n\\nMain visible finding: the figure presents X-Cell as a large-scale single-cell perturbation foundation model trained on multi-screen atlas data, designed for perturbation prediction and biological generalization, with X-Cell-Ultra shown as substantially larger than X-Cell.\",\ndocs/input-representation-atlas/data/atlas.json:32720:        \"page_no\": 4,\ndocs/input-representation-atlas/data/atlas.json:32721:        \"selection_score\": 364,\ndocs/input-representation-atlas/data/atlas.json:32722:        \"selection_reasons\": [\ndocs/input-representation-atlas/data/atlas.json:32723:          \"1 model-name tokens match\",\ndocs/input-representation-atlas/data/atlas.json:32724:          \"architecture/workflow figure\",\ndocs/input-representation-atlas/data/atlas.json:3