        \"text_native_token_stream\",\ndocs/input-representation-atlas/data/atlas.json:33677:        \"discrete_biological_symbol_stream\"\ndocs/input-representation-atlas/data/atlas.json:33678:      ],\ndocs/input-representation-atlas/data/atlas.json:33679:      \"subtypes\": [\ndocs/input-representation-atlas/data/atlas.json:33680:        \"multi_track_structural_symbol_stream\",\ndocs/input-representation-atlas/data/atlas.json:33681:        \"serialized_biological_context_or_ordered_profile\"\ndocs/input-representation-atlas/data/atlas.json:33682:      ],\ndocs/input-representation-atlas/data/atlas.json:33683:      \"modalities\": [\ndocs/input-representation-atlas/data/atlas.json:33684:        \"genetic perturbation transcriptomic responses\",\ndocs/input-representation-atlas/data/atlas.json:33685:        \"single-cell gene expression\",\ndocs/input-representation-atlas/data/atlas.json:33686:        \"single-cell perturbation transcriptomics\"\ndocs/input-representation-atlas/data/atlas.json:33687:      ],\ndocs/input-representation-atlas/data/atlas.json:33688:      \"lifecycle_phases\": [\ndocs/input-representation-atlas/data/atlas.json:33689:        \"evaluation\",\ndocs/input-representation-atlas/data/atlas.json:33690:        \"fine_tuning\",\ndocs/input-representation-atlas/data/atlas.json:33691:        \"pretraining\"\ndocs/input-representation-atlas/data/atlas.json:33692:      ],\ndocs/input-representation-atlas/data/atlas.json:33693:      \"fusion_topologies\": [\ndocs/input-representation-atlas/data/atlas.json:33694:        \"other_explicit\",\ndocs/input-representation-atlas/data/atlas.json:33695:        \"unclear\"\ndocs/input-representation-atlas/data/atlas.json:33696:      ],\ndocs/input-representation-atlas/data/atlas.json:33697:      \"text_roles\": [\ndocs/input-representation-atlas/data/atlas.json:33698:        \"biological_payload\"\ndocs/input-representation-atlas/data/atlas.json:33699:      ],\ndocs/input-representation-atlas/data/atlas.json:33700:      \"figure\": {\ndocs/input-representation-atlas/data/atlas.json:33701:        \"asset\": \"assets/figures/full_2026_07_06_rec_003517_figure_005.png\",\ndocs/input-representation-atlas/data/atlas.json:33702:        \"source_path\": \"data/docling_include_vlm_52_2026-07-10_nolimits/figures/full_2026_07_06_rec_003517/figure_005.png\",\ndocs/input-representation-atlas/data/atlas.json:33703:        \"figure_index\": 5,\ndocs/input-representation-atlas/data/atlas.json:33704:        \"caption\": \"Fig. 5 X-Cell-Ultra generalizes to melanocyte progenitors and primary human T cells in a zero-shot manner. (A) Schematic shows pre-training of X-Cell-Ultra on X-Atlas/Pisces datasets, excluding the melanocyte progenitor population from the iPSC Multi-Diff screen. We evaluated X-Cell-Ultra in a zero-shot manner on the melanocyte progenitor population as well as primary human T cell datasets from Zhu et al. [57]. We used test-time adaptation to calibrate the models on these datasets using self-supervised objectives. (B) Test loss follows a power law with trainable parameters ( L ( N ) ∝ N -0 . 03 , R 2 = 0 . 97; Table A4). Five models (83M-3.1B) were trained for 20 epochs on the Replogle-Nadig dataset under identical hyperparameters. Circle size is proportional to parameter count; color indicates DE Pearson r . Line plots (right) show how X-Cell-Architecture (blue) and X-Cell-Ultra-Architecture (pink) compare with respect to collapse metrics δ -norm ratio (top) and Pearson correlation of differentially expressed genes (bottom) on test set. (C) Performance of the control mean baseline, perturbation mean baseline, STATE, X-Cell, and X-Cell-Ultra with respect to Pearson ∆ (left), differential expression direction match (middle), and mean absolute error (right). Histogram (top) shows the metrics within 1,341 perturbations and the barplots (bottom) show mean ± 95% C.I. (D) Boxplots show the distribution of Pearson ∆ (left), differential expression direction match (middle), and mean absolute error (right) for 291 perturbations in resting and stimulated (2 time points) primary human T cells for 2 different donors. (E) Heatmaps show the log 2 fold change in expression of APPL2 in D2 (left) and D3 (right) 48 hours post-stimulation as observed by the data and predicted by STATE, X-Cell, and X-Cell-Ultra.\",\ndocs/input-representation-atlas/data/atlas.json:33705:        \"description\": \"SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\\n\\nMulti-panel scientific figure combining model/workflow schematic and quantitative benchmark plots for “X-Cell-Ultra.”\\n\\nPanel A shows a workflow/model diagram: perturbation data from cell lines and iPSC-derived cell types, pre-training, and genome-scale zero-shot inference for primary human T cells, including “test-time adaptation” with frozen cross-attention/reduced learning rate.\\n\\nPanel B shows model scaling dynamics: scatter/line plot of test loss versus trainable parameters for Replogle-Nadig, with Pearson r color scale, plus inset line plots comparing X-Cell and X-Cell-Ultra across epochs for Pearson correlation and differential expression Pearson correlation.\\n\\nPanels C and D show benchmark results with histograms and bar/box plots for Pearson Δ, differential expression direction match, and MAE. Labels include melanocyte progenitor test perturbations, D2/D3 rest and stimulation contexts, and baselines such as control mean, perturbation mean, STATE, X-Cell, and X-Cell-Ultra.\\n\\nPanel E shows clustered heatmaps of log fold-change values for APPL2 under D2 and D3 stimulation at 48 hours, comparing STATE, X-Cell, X-Cell-Ultra, and measured stimulation conditions.\\n\\nMain visible finding: X-Cell-Ultra generally improves predictive performance, with higher Pearson/direction-match metrics and lower MAE than X-Cell or baseline methods, and shows improved scaling with larger trainable parameter counts.\",\ndocs/input-representation-atlas/data/atlas.json:33706:        \"page_no\": 11,\ndocs/input-representation-atlas/data/atla